.th ODB I 1/26/76
.sh NAME
odb - on-line debugger
.sh SYNOPSIS
odb name [symfile]
.sh DESCRIPTION
odb is a QMC grown symbolic debugging system which operates in raw
mode.
If 'symfile' is given, the symbol table is obtained from it, otherwise
it is obtained
from the file being debugged. If the file being debugged does not
contain a symbol table, odb can still be used, however without
symbolic referencing.
.sp 1
odb uses RAW mode. When it is started it will print '*' as the
command level prompt character. A CTRL-D at command level will
return to shell. A '#' will erase the last typed character
like in cooked mode, the Rub-Out will erase the whole line.
.sp 1
The format for odb's requests is an address followed by a command
character.  Addresses are expressions and are formed from the
following :
.br
.in+15
.ti-3
1) The symbol '.' which indicates the current pointer of odb.
.ti-3
.br
2) A name whose value is obtained from the symbol table.
.br
.ti-3
3) An octal number. If a number contains an 8 or 9, result will
be unpredictable.
.br
.ti-3
4) A decimal number which consists of the digits 0-9 followed by
a decimal point.
.br
.ti-3
5) Arithmetic operators + and - to combine the operands.
.br
.ti-3
6) A "@" before an expression forms an expression whose value is
the number in the word addressed by the first expression.
.ti-3
.br
7) Expressions are evaluated left to right.
.br
.ti-3
8) ASCII value of any character if preceded by an apostrophe.
.br
.ti-3
9) ASCII value of two characters if preceded by a quotation mark
.br
.ti-3
10) Tabs and spaces may be used freely.
.in-15
.sp 1
Commands :
.br
.in+15
.ti-5
k/   open the word at location k, if odd address only the byte
is opened.
.br
.ti-5
 /   reopen the last opened location.
.br
.ti-5
.sp 1
a1,a2/ 	>filename (cr/lf)
.br
successive words, between addresses a1 and a2, are typed,
on the specified file. ">" is typed by odb. cr/lf must
terminate the command. If no file name is specified, the
output will be sent to the terminal. A Rub-Out during the
display causes a return to 'odb'.
.br
.ti-5
k\\   open the byte at location k.
.br
.ti-5
 \\   reopen the last opened byte.
.br
.ti-5
.sp 1
a1,a2\\ >filename (cr/lf)
.br
similar to "a1,a2/", but bytes will be displayed. 
.br
.ti-5
lf   open the next sequential location.
.br
.ti-5
^    open the previous location.
.br
If opened location was a byte, the lf or the ^ opens the next
byte.
.br
.ti-5
cr   close open location and wait at command level.
.br
.ti-5
<    contents of opened word is indexed by contents of PC, and
that location is opened. It only operates on words,
(useful for assembler programs).
.br
.ti-5
>    contents of opened word is taken as a relative branch
instruction and referenced location is opened. Only
operates on words,(useful for assembler programs).
.br
.ti-5
*    contents of the opened word is the address of the next
location that is opened.  Only operates on words.
.br
.ti-5
=    the value of the expression preceding the command is
calculated and typed. If no expression is typed the
current value of the location pointer is displayed.
This command does not effect the value of the ".".
.br
.ti-5
:    instruction type-in command. See below for details.
.br
.ti-5
(ACC)t(CH)
.br
where CH is any command character, causes 'odb' to type a ">"
on the next line. The user may then type a filename followed
by (cr/lf) to type-out the Symbol Table, if exists, to the file
or just a (cr/lf) if the Table is to go to the terminal.
A Rub-Out during the display causes a return to 'odb'.
.br
.ti-5
!cr  will result in the display of the registers.
.br
.ti-5
!r   open register r, where r may be either a general register (0-7)
or the program status register (s).
.br
.ti-5
%    the control is transferred to UNIX. The remainder of
the line is interpreted as a command by UNIX.
.br
.ti-5
^D (control+D)
.br
quits the debugger.
.in-15
.sp 1
Modifications to the opened location can be made before a lf, cr,
^, <, > or * is typed. Any legal expression may be typed to
modify an opened location.
.sp 1
Instruction Type-IN :
.br
After a location has been opened ":" command may be followed
with an instruction and a (cr/lf) to deposit the instruction
there. After the ":" command 'odb' temporarily uses cooked
mode. Therefore '#' & '@' must be treated accordingly.
All the instruction modes are accepted. However, in register
mode use r6 (or %6) for 'sp' & r7 (or %7) for 'pc'.
.sp 1
Breakpoints :
.br
A breakpoint may be set at any instruction. Up to 9 breakpoints
may be set, at any one time, numbered 0 through 8.
.in+15
.br
.ti-8
k;b	will set the next 'available' breakpoint at location k.
.br
.ti-8
k;nb	will set the breakpoint 'n' at location k.
.br
.ti-8
;b	will remove all breakpoints.
.br
.ti-8
;nb	will remove the 'n-th' breakpoint.
.br
.ti-15
e.g.
.sp 1
.ti-8
*200;b	sets breakpoint 0 at location 200.
.br
.ti-8
*400;b	sets breakpoint 1 at location 400.
.br
.ti-8
*500;1b	resets breakpoint 1.
.br
.ti-8
*;0b	removes the 0-th breakpoint.
.br
.in-15
.sp 1
The command !b/ opens the location containing the address of
breakpoint 0. Internally 3 locations are reserved for each
breakpoint. The first holds the address of the breakpoint,
next one holds it's contents and the third one holds the
repeat count for that breakpoint. After typing !b/ the other
breakpoints may be inspected by typing Line-Feeds. However
their contents may not be modified in the usual manner. The
commands mentioned above, should be used to set, change or
remove breakpoints.
.br
.sp 1
Running The Program :
.br
.sp 1
Odb uses cooked mode during the execution.
.br
.in+15
.ti-8
k;g	command is used to start execution at location k.
If k is omitted, the start address of the program is obtained
from the file's header.
Following "k;g" arguments may be given to execute the file.
.br
.ti-8
k;p	command is used to continue execution after having halted.
If k is present, it specifies the number of times the breakpoint
is to be encountered before program execution is suspended. If k
is omitted, the execution stops when next breakpoint is encountered.
.br
.ti-8
;t	will terminate the traced process.
.br
.in-15
.sp 1
When a breakpoint is encountered, execution stops and odb prints
Bn;addr (where n is the breakpoint number and addr is the address
of the breakpoint.).
.br
The execution of the program will also stop if any signal generated,
odb will announce the reason.
.br
When the program is in a stop state, the registers and the locations
may be examined and modified, if desired. However, any alterations
made to the locations will be to the core image of the running
process. Thus the original file being debugged will not be affected.
.br
.sp 1
Type-out Modes:
.br
odb's  original type-out mode is octal. However this,at any time,
can be changed to any one of the following modes :
.in+15
.br
.ti-8
(ACC)d  changes the current mode to decimal.
.br
.ti-8
(ACC)c  changes the current mode to character.
.br
.ti-8
(ACC)o  changes the current mode to octal.
.br
.ti-8
(ACC)i  mode is changed to intruction.  The addressed
locations will be interpreted as PDP11 instructions.
.in-15
.sp 1
If any of the above commands is immediately folllowed by a ';',
while a location is open, the contents of the open location is
re-displayed in the new mode.
.sh DIAGNOSTICS
? for incorrect selection of a command or an illegal memory
reference.
.sh "SEE ALSO"
db(I), cdb(I), core(V), a.out(V)
.sh BUGS
